WEBVTT

00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:07.863
Soft tree ensembles are gradient-trained decision
trees that rival neural networks on tabular data,

00:00:07.943 --> 00:00:10.644
yet their training has lacked theory.

00:00:10.724 --> 00:00:16.057
This ICLR 2022 paper introduces the Tree Neural
Tangent Kernel, or TNTK,

00:00:16.137 --> 00:00:20.041
describing an ensemble of infinitely many soft trees.

00:00:20.121 --> 00:00:24.176
With it, the authors prove global training convergence,

00:00:24.256 --> 00:00:27.258
show oblivious trees match ordinary ones,

00:00:27.338 --> 00:00:31.919
and reveal that very deep trees degenerate into a
flat kernel,

00:00:31.999 --> 00:00:34.400
all validated on ninety datasets.

00:00:34.780 --> 00:00:40.794
Tree ensembles and neural networks are two of the
most widely used model families.

00:00:40.874 --> 00:00:46.516
A soft tree is a decision tree whose splitting
rules are made differentiable,

00:00:46.596 --> 00:00:51.793
so the whole model trains by gradient descent
instead of greedy search.

00:00:51.873 --> 00:00:55.212
Ensembles of soft trees excel on tabular data,

00:00:55.292 --> 00:01:01.306
and practitioners rely on tricks like parameter
sharing, adjusting split hardness,

00:01:01.386 --> 00:01:03.238
and over-parameterization.

00:01:03.318 --> 00:01:06.508
Yet almost no theory explains why they work.

00:01:06.588 --> 00:01:08.440
This paper fills that gap.

00:01:08.820 --> 00:01:15.012
The Neural Tangent Kernel has become a powerful
tool for understanding neural networks

00:01:15.092 --> 00:01:17.491
with infinitely many hidden nodes.

00:01:17.571 --> 00:01:23.471
It has been derived for multi-layer perceptrons,
convolutional networks, and more,

00:01:23.551 --> 00:01:26.242
each yielding its own distinct kernel.

00:01:26.322 --> 00:01:29.815
But no one had derived an NTK for tree ensembles.

00:01:29.895 --> 00:01:34.337
Because soft trees inherit characteristics of
neural networks,

00:01:34.417 --> 00:01:38.275
the authors saw the NTK framework as the natural lens,

00:01:38.355 --> 00:01:42.650
seeking a closed-form kernel for infinitely many
soft trees.

00:01:43.030 --> 00:01:46.616
The paper makes four contributions.

00:01:46.696 --> 00:01:50.282
First,

00:01:50.362 --> 00:01:53.947
it derives the Tree Neural Tangent Kernel at
initialization for infinitely many perfect

00:01:54.028 --> 00:01:57.613
binary trees of arbitrary depth,

00:01:57.693 --> 00:02:01.279
and proves the kernel stays essentially constant
during training,

00:02:01.359 --> 00:02:04.945
enabling analysis as kernel regression and a proof
of global convergence.

00:02:05.025 --> 00:02:08.611
Second, it shows oblivious ensembles,

00:02:08.691 --> 00:02:12.277
which share splitting rules within each depth as in NODE,

00:02:12.357 --> 00:02:15.943
converge to the very same kernel.

00:02:16.023 --> 00:02:19.608
Third, it characterizes the decision function,

00:02:19.688 --> 00:02:23.274
nearly linear in the basic case and more nonlinear
as splits harden.

00:02:23.354 --> 00:02:26.940
Fourth, it uncovers a degeneracy where deep trees
flatten the kernel.

00:02:27.320 --> 00:02:30.629
The method takes the number of trees to infinity.

00:02:30.709 --> 00:02:33.465
For perfect binary soft trees of depth d,

00:02:33.545 --> 00:02:39.621
the authors prove the Tree Neural Tangent Kernel
converges to a deterministic kernel with

00:02:39.701 --> 00:02:44.463
two parts: one from the inner splitting nodes and
one from the leaves.

00:02:44.543 --> 00:02:47.507
The soft split uses a scaled error function,

00:02:47.587 --> 00:02:50.896
a smooth sigmoid whose sharpness is set by alpha,

00:02:50.976 --> 00:02:55.461
and its expectations have closed forms, so the
kernel is analytic.

00:02:55.541 --> 00:02:59.335
Because the kernel stays constant and positive definite,

00:02:59.415 --> 00:03:03.623
training reduces to kernel regression with global
convergence,

00:03:03.703 --> 00:03:08.880
and the formula is not recursive in depth, so its
cost is depth-independent.

00:03:09.260 --> 00:03:12.261
The empirical study uses two kinds of data.

00:03:12.341 --> 00:03:13.622
For generalization,

00:03:13.702 --> 00:03:19.498
the authors run kernel regression with the Tree NTK
on ninety real-world datasets,

00:03:19.578 --> 00:03:25.875
comparing against the kernel of an infinitely wide
multi-layer perceptron and the classic

00:03:25.955 --> 00:03:27.953
radial basis function kernel.

00:03:28.033 --> 00:03:31.321
Tree depth is tuned from one up to twenty-nine,

00:03:31.401 --> 00:03:35.262
and the split-hardness parameter alpha is swept widely.

00:03:35.342 --> 00:03:37.626
For measuring computational cost,

00:03:37.706 --> 00:03:43.430
they use a synthetic dataset of three hundred
samples with ten Gaussian features.

00:03:43.810 --> 00:03:46.688
The experiments confirm every prediction.

00:03:46.768 --> 00:03:48.780
As the number of trees grows,

00:03:48.860 --> 00:03:53.036
the empirical kernel converges to the closed-form
Tree NTK,

00:03:53.116 --> 00:03:56.354
and training dynamics match kernel regression.

00:03:56.434 --> 00:04:00.322
Making trees deeper first improves then hurts accuracy,

00:04:00.402 --> 00:04:02.702
exactly the predicted degeneracy.

00:04:02.782 --> 00:04:07.968
On the ninety datasets the multi-layer perceptron
kernel wins on average,

00:04:08.048 --> 00:04:12.729
but the tree kernel is better on more than thirty
percent of them,

00:04:12.809 --> 00:04:15.254
where the tree inductive bias fits.

00:04:15.334 --> 00:04:20.520
And because its cost never grows with depth, it is
far faster to compute.

00:04:20.900 --> 00:04:22.723
Two ablations are central.

00:04:22.803 --> 00:04:29.164
Sweeping the split-hardness parameter alpha shows
harder splits give better dataset-wise

00:04:29.244 --> 00:04:31.579
win rates against the MLP kernel,

00:04:31.659 --> 00:04:39.118
climbing from about fourteen percent when splits
are soft to nearly thirty-five percent at the hardest,

00:04:39.198 --> 00:04:42.411
all above the radial basis function baseline.

00:04:42.491 --> 00:04:46.217
Sweeping tree depth reproduces the degeneracy story.

00:04:46.297 --> 00:04:51.633
The authors also verify empirically that oblivious
trees, as used by NODE,

00:04:51.713 --> 00:04:55.000
induce the same kernel as ordinary soft trees.

00:04:55.380 --> 00:04:57.693
A few numbers capture the impact.

00:04:57.773 --> 00:05:00.159
Across ninety real-world datasets,

00:05:00.239 --> 00:05:06.250
the tree kernel beats the multi-layer perceptron
kernel on more than thirty percent,

00:05:06.330 --> 00:05:10.166
peaking near thirty-five percent when splits are hard,

00:05:10.246 --> 00:05:14.880
versus under twelve percent for the radial basis
function kernel.

00:05:14.960 --> 00:05:19.739
Most striking, the tree kernel's cost is
independent of tree depth,

00:05:19.819 --> 00:05:22.422
while the MLP kernel scales linearly.

00:05:22.502 --> 00:05:25.540
The depths studied span one to twenty-nine.

00:05:25.920 --> 00:05:30.478
The lasting takeaway is that the neural tangent
kernel framework,

00:05:30.558 --> 00:05:34.688
once used only for neural networks, applies to tree
models.

00:05:34.768 --> 00:05:41.965
The resulting Tree NTK explains many soft-tree
behaviors from one theory: training converges globally,

00:05:42.045 --> 00:05:45.176
oblivious sharing costs nothing in the limit,

00:05:45.256 --> 00:05:48.886
deep trees degenerate into a nearly constant kernel,

00:05:48.966 --> 00:05:52.811
and harder splits yield a more useful nonlinear kernel.

00:05:52.891 --> 00:05:57.520
It also delivers a fast kernel whose cost is
independent of depth.
